      "id": "mysql-cluster-subops-guarded",
      "service": "database/mysql",
      "enabled": true,
      "heavy": true,
      "_note": "COVERAGE-ONLY guarded lifecycle for the mysql DBaaS cluster sub-op WRITE endpoints that the real heavy `database-mysql-cluster` lifecycle does NOT exercise (replicas, restore, promote, switchover, sync-state, log-export-configs, security-group-rules, backup-histories, kernel-upgrade, patch, add-block-storages, reset/sync replica state). It does NOT provision a real billable cluster. It soft-captures an EXISTING cluster_id from the read-only list; if none exists the {cluster_id} (and {instance_group_id}/{origin_cluster_id}/{replica_cluster_id}/{log_type}) path tokens still normalize to the catalog '*' key, so the endpoint is CALLED and RECORDED (a 404/400/403/409/422 counts as covered per the engine). Every mutating step is optional + grouped + broad expect_status so an isolated 4xx records coverage WITHOUT failing the run or chaining a real create->mutate->delete. heavy:true gates it behind SCP_RUN_HEAVY; DELETEs are destructive:true (SCP_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE). Bodies are best-effort from path/field semantics + the shared SCP DBaaS shape (data/api_bodies.json only has createcluster) and are UNPROVEN — promote to validated after a real 2xx against a dedicated test cluster. Shared DBaaS body shape across mysql/mariadb/epas/postgresql is identical here; sqlserver/cachestore diverge (see those fragments).",
